WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.450
Home first aid, danger signs, and earning from your hygiene skills.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.040
This is the video for module 4 of Home Hygiene and Sanitation.

3
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:17.520
Many child poisonings happen because a chemical was poured into a soft drink or water bottle, and a child drank it.

4
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.460
So keep every chemical in its original labelled container.

5
00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.470
The label tells a health worker what was swallowed.

6
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.230
Store chemicals high up or in a locked cupboard, out of children's sight and reach.

7
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.970
Keep them away from food and drinking water.

8
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:41.920
Close caps tightly. Never mix cleaning products, and never reuse a chemical container for water or food.

9
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:48.840
For a burn from hot water or oil, cool it under cool, clean running water for a long time.

10
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.250
Remove rings near the burn, and cover it loosely with a clean cloth.

11
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.330
Never apply oil, palm oil, toothpaste, raw egg or ice.

12
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.570
A burn larger than the person's palm, or on a baby, needs a health facility.

13
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:12.350
If a chemical is swallowed, do not make the person vomit, and do not give oil. Go to the nearest health facility immediately with the container.

14
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:22.110
For a chemical in the eye, rinse straight away with plenty of clean running water, holding the eyelids open, and keep rinsing on the way to care.

15
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:30.570
WHO defines diarrhoea as 3 or more loose or liquid stools a day, or more often than normal for that person.

16
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:37.920
The main danger is dehydration, losing water and salts, which can become serious quickly in a small child.

17
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:45.400
Start oral rehydration solution, ORS, mixed exactly as the sachet says with safe water.

18
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.920
Keep feeding, and keep breastfeeding. Do not stop food to rest the stomach.

19
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:57.050
Never give medicines, including antibiotics, without a health worker's advice.

20
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.820
Check for danger signs every few hours.

21
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.300
Blood in the stool.

22
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.230
Diarrhoea for many days.

23
00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:09.460
Signs of dehydration, like sunken eyes, being very sleepy, or little urine.

24
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:15.420
Large amounts of watery diarrhoea, or several people ill at once, which can be cholera.

25
00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:20.740
Or fever after contact with rats, which needs early testing for Lassa fever.

26
00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:29.480
For example, Fatima in Sokoto logged her daughter's loose stools: 4 on Monday, 3 on Tuesday, with ORS and breastfeeding.

27
00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:34.500
On Wednesday she saw blood, and went to the health centre that morning with her log.

28
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.760
Everything in this course is a skill people pay for.

29
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.040
But many new cleaners quote a figure that sounds fair and later find they made nothing.

30
00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:51.000
For example, Blessing in Abuja visits a flat first, and estimates 5 hours for herself and one helper.

31
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.560
Supplies cost about 2,000 naira, and transport about 1,500.

32
00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:05.640
Labour is 2 people for 5 hours at 800 naira an hour: 8,000 naira. She pays herself too.

33
00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:14.680
She sets aside 500 naira for equipment wear. Her total cost is 12,000 naira. These prices are approximate.

34
00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.060
Her cost-based price is the minimum she can accept.

35
00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.900
She adds a profit of 25% on cost, which is 3,000 naira.

36
00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:31.400
She quotes 15,000 naira in writing, listing what is included and what is not.

37
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:40.090
If she does 8 jobs like this a month, her profit is 24,000 naira, after paying everyone, including herself.

38
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:50.830
Check your price against the market, and save by grouping jobs. But never skip gloves or dilute products below the label strength. Customers notice.

39
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.090
To remember.

40
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.040
Lock chemicals away in their own containers.

41
00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.160
Cool burns with running water.

42
00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.790
Never make a poisoned person vomit.

43
00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.220
Give ORS and keep feeding, and go at once for any danger sign.

44
00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.790
And cost every job, including your own labour, before you add profit.

45
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.360
Make no health claims you cannot support.

46
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:23.960
Now do your home chemical and first aid check, make your family health watch card and log, and cost and quote your first cleaning job. Continue with the lessons below.
